--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FFmpeg, x264, x265 -- GPL-2.0-or-later
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Remotion's compositor package (@remotion/compositor-*) bundles an FFmpeg built
with --enable-gpl, --enable-libx264 and --enable-libx265. Those binaries and the
libav* shared libraries beside them are covered by the GNU General Public
License, version 2 or later.

These binaries are NOT part of this repository. They are downloaded by
`npm install` as part of Remotion's platform-specific package.

This matters when binaries are distributed, not when source is:

  * Publishing or cloning THIS SOURCE REPOSITORY carries no GPL obligation,
    because no GPL code is contained in it.

  * Distributing a BUILT APPLICATION (a .dmg, a GitHub release asset, an app
    store submission) does distribute those GPL binaries. Whoever does that
    becomes a GPL distributor and must comply -- which includes offering the
    corresponding source of FFmpeg and its GPL dependencies to every recipient,
    and imposing no further restrictions on the rights the GPL grants them.

Which version of the GPL applies, and what a source offer has to be
---------------------------------------------------------------------

These binaries are "GPL-2.0-or-later", so a distributor may elect version 3.
Anyone distributing a build of Talkie should elect GPL-3.0-or-later and say so,
because only version 3 accepts a network location as the way to provide source.
GPLv2 section 3 does not: it wants the source to accompany the binary, or a
written offer valid for three years. A URL alone does not satisfy it.

The links below are upstream release pages, not corresponding source. The GPL
asks for the source of the binary actually shipped -- FFmpeg n7.1 as Remotion
configured and built it, with its exact flags -- so a distributor has to publish
that tree, or a tarball of it, beside the download. `ffmpeg -buildconf` on the
binary in @remotion/compositor-darwin-arm64 prints the configuration it was
built with.

FFmpeg source:  https://ffmpeg.org/download.html
x264 source:    https://www.videolan.org/developers/x264.html
x265 source:    https://www.videolan.org/developers/x265.html

Talkie itself does not use the GPL encoders. Recording and export both ask for
h264_videotoolbox, the hardware encoder built into every Mac it runs on. That
does not make the shipped binary any less GPL -- it was linked with x264 and
x265 and stays covered however it is invoked -- but it means no feature depends
on them, and a build of FFmpeg without --enable-gpl would serve Talkie equally.

Two things worth settling with Remotion before distributing any build:

  * Their FFmpeg is configured --enable-gpl together with --enable-libfdk-aac.
    fdk-aac's licence is not GPL-compatible, and FFmpeg's own build rules
    require --enable-nonfree for that combination, which produces a binary
    marked as not redistributable. The buildconf shows no --enable-nonfree.

  * Whether a build without --enable-gpl, --enable-libx264 and --enable-libx265
    is available, which would remove this section's obligations entirely.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
eSpeak NG -- GPL-3.0-or-later, inside the Kokoro voice
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Kokoro voice turns text into phonemes with the `phonemizer` npm package.
That package's own licence field says Apache-2.0, but it carries eSpeak NG
compiled to WebAssembly (dist/phonemizer.*), and eSpeak NG is licensed under
the GNU General Public License, version 3 or later.

Talkie keeps it at arm's length. kokoro-js, phonemizer, transformers.js and ONNX
Runtime are loaded only by a separate worker program (out/main/kokoroWorker.cjs)
that the app starts as its own process and exchanges plain messages with -- the
same footing as the GPL FFmpeg above. Nothing in the main process, the windows
or the MCP server imports them; src/main/tts/kokoro/kokoroBoundary.test.ts fails
the build if anything does. Remotion, whose licence is not GPL-compatible, is
never in the same program as eSpeak NG.

As with FFmpeg, this binds distributors of a BUILT APPLICATION, not this source
repository, which contains no eSpeak NG code. Anyone distributing a build has to
offer the corresponding source of the eSpeak NG that ships in it.

Open item before distributing any build: phonemizer does not say which eSpeak NG
version it compiled, or with which changes, and the GPL asks for the source of
the binary actually shipped. Settle that with the phonemizer.js maintainers
(https://github.com/xenova/phonemizer.js), or build the phonemizer from a known
eSpeak NG release, before publishing a .dmg.

eSpeak NG source:  https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng

The Kokoro model weights themselves (hexgrad/Kokoro-82M, converted to ONNX by
onnx-community) are Apache-2.0. They are not shipped in the app: Talkie
downloads them from Hugging Face the first time Kokoro is chosen, pinned to one
revision and checked against its published SHA-256.

